<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#riesling">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Riesling</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Riesling is a white grape variety native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, prized for aromatic, high-acid wines ranging from dry to sweet. Its earliest known written record is a 1435 cellar account of Count John IV of Katzenelnbogen at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russelsheim">Rüsselsheim</a>. It became the leading grape of the Rhine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and is now grown widely, notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/riesling.jpg" alt="Riesling" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: T.o.m. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riesling_grapes_leaves.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Riesling was long said to descend from wild vines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> region, without much support for the claim. DNA fingerprinting by Ferdinand Regner showed that one parent is Gouais blanc, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as Weißer Heunisch, a variety widely grown by French and German peasants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The other parent is a cross between a wild vine and Traminer. Because both parents have a long history in Germany, the cross is presumed to have happened in the Rhine valley, though it could have occurred anywhere between the parents' homelands.</p><p>The modern spelling Riesling was first documented in 1552, in Hieronymus Bock's Latin herbal. A local claim that the name comes from a stream and vineyard called Ritzling in the Wachau has no documentary evidence behind it. A red-skinned version, Roter Riesling, is very rare; whether it is a mutation of the white grape or its forerunner is unsettled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Riesling is an aromatic white grape with flowery aromas and high acidity. It is made into dry, semi-sweet, sweet and sparkling wines, usually from the one variety and seldom aged in oak. Its character depends strongly on where it is grown: wines from cool regions such as the Mosel often show apple and tree-fruit notes, while warmer sites bring citrus and peach. Its acidity suits long ageing, and old wines may take on a petrol note caused by the compound TDN.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> the prädikat system classifies wines by must weight and sweetness, from dry to the beerenauslese and trockenbeerenauslese dessert wines. The use of noble rot in Riesling is traced to the late 18th century at Schloss Johannisberg, where permission from the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> to start picking arrived too late and the rotted grapes still made excellent wine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Riesling is the leading grape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and is also the basis of its sparkling Sekt. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> over a fifth of the vineyards carry it. In 1838 William Macarthur planted vines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a> in New South Wales, and it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s most planted white grape until the early 1990s. German immigrants took it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the late nineteenth century, with plantings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> by 1857 and Washington in 1871.</p><p>German breeders also used it for crosses. Müller-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a>, developed at the Geisenheim institute in 1882, is a cross of Riesling and Madeleine Royale, and Kerner crosses it with Trollinger. Several grapes with Riesling in their names, such as Welschriesling, are unrelated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Riesling” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
